<h2>When expert opinion is relevant</h2>
<p>The Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, which has replaced the Evidence Act, 1872, carries forward the familiar rule that when the court has to form an opinion upon a point of foreign law, science or art, or the identity of handwriting or finger impressions, the opinions of persons specially skilled in those fields are relevant. The provision that practitioners knew as Section 45 of the Evidence Act now lives on, in substance, in the new code, and its logic in commercial litigation is unchanged: where the disputed question requires specialised knowledge, the court may receive, though never surrender to, expert opinion.</p>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Recurring question in commercial suits</th><th>Typical expert</th></tr>
<tr><td>Disputed signatures, interpolation, age of ink or writing</td><td>Handwriting and questioned-document examiner</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accounts, diversion of funds, quantification of loss, valuation of shares or a business</td><td>Chartered accountant, forensic auditor or registered valuer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Content of foreign law governing a contract</td><td>Practitioner or academic qualified in that legal system</td></tr>
<tr><td>Engineering failures, construction defects, delay analysis</td><td>Engineer or technical delay analyst</td></tr>
<tr><td>Authenticity and forensics of digital records, emails, metadata</td><td>Digital forensics examiner, with the certification the law requires for electronic records</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Two routes to the expertise</h2>
<p>Expert input reaches a commercial suit by two distinct routes, and the difference matters tactically. The first is the party-appointed expert: a witness retained by one side who prepares a report and enters the witness box for that party. The second is the court-appointed commissioner. Order XXVI Rule 10A of the Code of Civil Procedure empowers the court, where a question arising in a suit involves any scientific investigation which cannot conveniently be conducted before the court, to issue a commission to a person it considers appropriate, directing an inquiry and a report.</p>
<div class="note"><p>The court-appointed route carries a structural advantage of neutrality: the commissioner answers to the court, not to a party. But it is not a substitute for the parties' own evidentiary burden. A litigant who bears the onus on a technical issue should not assume the court will appoint a commissioner; the safer course is to build the party expert case and to invite a commission where an independent examination — of a machine, a site, a server or a disputed document — would assist.</p></div>

<h2>How expert evidence is led</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep">The report and its foundations. The expert prepares a signed report stating qualifications, the questions put, the materials examined, the methodology and the conclusions. The underlying data — worksheets, test results, source documents — should accompany it, because an opinion divorced from its data is nearly weightless.</div>
<div class="fstep">Disclosure on the case-management schedule. In commercial suits the court fixes timelines for evidence at the case-management hearing, and expert reports belong on that schedule. Springing a report at trial invites exclusion or an adjournment with costs.</div>
<div class="fstep">Affidavit of examination-in-chief. The expert files an affidavit in lieu of examination-in-chief, proving the report and exhibiting it along with the underlying material.</div>
<div class="fstep">Cross-examination. The opposite party tests the expert on qualifications, method, data and independence. Re-examination, if any, is confined to clarifying matters arising from the cross.</div>
</div>
<p>Where the expert opines on facts he did not personally observe — a valuation resting on management accounts, a delay analysis resting on site records — those primary facts must be proved by witnesses who can speak to them. The expert supplies inference, not proof of the substratum.</p>

<h2>The opinion is advisory, the judgment is judicial</h2>
<p>Courts have consistently treated expert testimony as opinion evidence of an advisory character. The expert equips the judge with the criteria of the discipline so that the judge can form an independent conclusion; the expert does not decide the issue. It follows that a court may prefer one expert to another for articulated reasons, may reject both where the reasoning is unsound, and may act on an opinion only when it is corroborated by, or at least consistent with, the remaining evidence. In handwriting matters in particular, courts approach uncorroborated opinion with caution and look for supporting circumstances before finding forgery proved.</p>

<h2>Attacking the other side's expert</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h4>Qualifications</h4><p>Specialised skill in the precise field is what makes the opinion relevant. A generalist opining on a niche question, or credentials that do not survive scrutiny, undercut admissibility and weight.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Methodology</h4><p>Was the method one accepted in the discipline, and was it actually followed? Deviations, shortcuts and unexplained assumptions are the richest seam in cross-examination.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Data</h4><p>An opinion is only as good as its inputs. Incomplete records, unverified copies, or reliance on instructions from the client rather than source material can dismantle a confident conclusion.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Independence</h4><p>Fee arrangements contingent on outcome, prior alignment with the party, or advocacy dressed as analysis all invite the court to discount the report.</p></div>
</div>
<p>In a well-managed commercial suit, expert evidence is planned at the pleading stage: the technical issues are identified, the right discipline is chosen, the report is disclosed on schedule with its data, and the substratum facts are proved independently. That preparation, more than the eminence of the signatory, is what makes an expert opinion carry weight when judgment is written.</p>
<p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice or a solicitation.</p>
